INT. NIGHT TRAIN - DINING CAR - SNOWSTORM

White-clothed tables, brass lamps, windows frosted from outside. The car sways. A candle gutters in every glass. At the far end, a tarnished samovar steams.

Only a handful of passengers remain. A WOMAN (30s, trench coat, damp hair) sits alone at a two-top, a closed book in front of her. She hasn't turned a page in twenty minutes.

CALLUM FAIRBROTHER (30s, rumpled suit, tie loosened) stands at the doorway, scanning for a seat. The car is nearly empty and every table is free. He walks to hers anyway.

CALLUM
Is this taken?

WOMAN
There are fourteen empty tables.

CALLUM
I can count.

She almost smiles. Gestures to the chair. He sits.

GIORGOS ANAGNOSTOU (50s, waiter, white jacket, impeccable mustache) glides over with a menu.

GIORGOS
Madam, sir. Tonight we have lamb or lamb.

CALLUM
Lamb.

WOMAN
The same.

GIORGOS
A wise choice. Wine?

CALLUM
Red.

WOMAN
Whatever he's having.

Giorgos nods and withdraws. Callum straightens his cutlery. She watches him do it.

WOMAN (CONT'D)
You're nervous.

CALLUM
I'm on a train in a blizzard with a stranger.

WOMAN
Nadia.

CALLUM
Pardon?

NADIA
My name. Nadia Haddad. Now I'm not a stranger.

CALLUM
Callum.

NADIA
That's a Scottish name.

CALLUM
My mother was Scottish. My father was an accountant.

NADIA
And you?

CALLUM
Also an accountant.

NADIA
Ah. That explains the cutlery.

They both laugh. The train lurches. Candles flicker. A glass slides and Callum catches it without looking, almost gracefully.

Behind them, an ELDERLY COUPLE enters: HEDDA LINDQVIST-ROSE (70s, pearls, cane) and AMBROSE (70s, bow tie, a flask) bickering softly in tandem.

HEDDA
Ambrose, you promised no more than one.

AMBROSE
I promised no more than one at a time.

HEDDA
That's not what a promise is.

AMBROSE
It's what a promise is when you've been married fifty years.

They pass the table. Hedda stops.

HEDDA
My dears, you make a handsome pair.

CALLUM
Oh, we're not--

NADIA
We just met.

AMBROSE
That's how it starts.

HEDDA
That's exactly how it starts.

They settle at the table behind them, still murmuring. Nadia lowers her voice.

NADIA
They're adorable.

CALLUM
They're terrifying.

Giorgos returns with wine. Pours two glasses, then pauses over the third table.

GIORGOS
Sir, madam, may I recommend the plum tart? It is the only thing I make myself.

HEDDA
Two, please.

AMBROSE
Three. For the road.

GIORGOS
We are on the train, sir.

AMBROSE
Even so.

Callum raises his glass to Nadia. She lifts hers. Their eyes catch, hold.

CALLUM
To blizzards.

NADIA
To stranded trains.

HEDDA
^To fifty years!

AMBROSE
^To another fifty!

NADIA
(laughing)
To everyone.

Giorgos steps back. The couple chatters. The train rocks. For a moment the whole car glows.

Then the lights flicker and die. Darkness. A scream of brakes. The car lurches. Glass shatters.

CALLUM
Nadia?

NADIA
I'm here.

CALLUM
Hold on to me.

A hand finds a hand in the dark.

HEDDA (O.S.)
Ambrose!

AMBROSE (O.S.)
Right here, love.

GIORGOS (O.S.)
Please remain seated.

The train shudders to a stop. Snow hisses against the windows. Somewhere, a single match is struck, and a tiny flame blooms.

NADIA
Callum.

CALLUM
Yes?

NADIA
I think I'd like to know your last name.

CALLUM
Fairbrother.

NADIA
That is a terrible name.

CALLUM
I'm told it's improving.
